WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.000
The Moon holds traces of fire. Mars holds traces of water.

2
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:17.000
Was Mars ever wet?

3
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:27.000
In July 2003, Opportunity launched toward Mars.

4
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:42.000
Airbags cushioned the landing in Eagle Crater.

5
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Designed for 90 sols, the rover began studying layered rocks.

6
00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:06.000
It found tiny spherules, nicknamed blueberries.

7
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Cameras, a microscope, RAT and spectrometers helped investigate.

8
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:30.000
At El Capitan, it observed sulfates and traces of salt crystals.

9
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:43.000
Analysis of the spherules revealed hematite.

10
00:01:43.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Water may have helped spherules grow; erosion exposed them.

11
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:12.000
Different explanations were compared. Water was the best fit.

12
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:25.000
Opportunity covered more than 45 km in nearly 15 years.

13
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:42.000
A global dust storm cut its power. Last contact was June 10, 2018.

14
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:48.000
Now it is your turn to investigate the rocks of Mars.
